            <ArticleTitle>Risk factors for headache chronification.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>16-25</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4610.2007.00970.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>About 4% of the adult population and about 1% to 2% of children experience chronic attacks on a daily or near daily basis. While there is uncertainty about the biological mechanisms that lead to headache &quot;chronification,&quot; the epidemiologic literature has provided some insight into modifiable and nonmodifiable factors that appear to influence risk of headache progression. This review summarizes the evidence from population-based studies related to the chronic daily headache phenotype, natural history, and risk factors that may influence incidence, prevalence, or prognosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
